"Enhancing Space-Time Adaptive Processing Through the Slepian Transform1 | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore (https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/9455297)\nciteturn225search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: last week; A High Dynamic Range Space-Time Adaptive Processing (HDR-STAP) improves cancellation of large interference sources by using a Fast Slepian Transform to capture and cancel more clutter energy than other STAP approaches.\n\n# Enhancing Space-Time Adaptive Processing Through the Slepian Transform^{1}\n\nPublisher: IEEE\n\n## Abstract:\n\nA High Dynamic Range Space-Time Adaptive Processing (HDR-STAP) improves cancellation of large interference sources by using a Fast Slepian Transform to capture and cancel...Show More\n\n## Metadata\n\n## Abstract:\n\nA High Dynamic Range Space-Time Adaptive Processing (HDR-STAP) improves cancellation of large interference sources by using a Fast Slepian Transform to capture and cancel more clutter energy than other STAP approaches. STAP’s efficacy relies on its ability to estimate clutter interference across space (antenna elements) and time (pulses) but hits limitations due to interference strength and spectrum spread. In addition to clutter cancellation limits, the target signal can suffer a STAP processing loss. This paper focuses on the signal to clutter enhancement metric to compare performance of different STAP techniques. HDR-STAP projects the estimated clutter covariance matrix onto the Slepian basis functions. A limited number of orthogonal Slepian basis functions are defined using the signals’ sample rate and a selected finite bandwidth. HDR-STAP takes advantage of a Fast Slepian transform (FST) from Karnik, et. all [1], producing a practical implementation. This article demonstrates that HDR-STAP removes more clutter than sample matrix inversion (SMI) and Eigendecomposition STAP while achieving signal losses below those predicted by the Reed, Mallet, and Brennon (RMB) rule.\n\nPublished in: 2021 IEEE Radar Conference (RadarConf21)\n\nDate of Conference: 07-14 May 2021\n\nDate Added to IEEE Xplore: 18 June 2021\n\nISBN Information:\n\nISSN Information:\n\nDOI: 10.1109/RadarConf2147009.2021.9455297\n\nPublisher: IEEE\n\nConference Location: Atlanta, GA, USA--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSLEPIAN ARRAY PROCESSING: CONCEPTS AND PRACTICAL (https://repository.gatech.edu/bitstreams/2d7c903e-3522-41d4-bf49-dcbe877737cb/download)\nciteturn225search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; broadband signals based on a union of Slepian spaces, which are more aptly suited fordealing with spectral leakage and dynamic range disparities. ... show how this method can be seamlessly applied to the problem of localization in multi-\nCHAPTER 3\nBROADBAND SOURCE LOCALIZATION\nIn this chapter, we extend classical greedy algorithms for sparse recovery (e.g., orthogo-\nnal matching pursuit) to localize broadband sources. We leverage models for samples of\nbroadband signals based on a union of Slepian spaces, which are more aptly suited for\ndealing with spectral leakage and dynamic range disparities. We show that by using these\nmodels, our adapted algorithms can successfully localize broadband sources under a vari-\nety of adverse operating scenarios. Furthermore, we show that our algorithms outperform\ncomplementary methods that use more standard Fourier models. The method is then easily\nextended to perform estimation from compressed measurements with little loss in fidelity\nas long as the number of measurements are on the order of the signal’s implicit degrees\nof freedom. Through a relatively straightforward change in domains (and perspectives) we\nshow how this method can be seamlessly applied to the problem of localization in multi-\nsensor arrays.\nThis chapter is organized as follows. In section 3.1 we formally introduce the topic of\nbroadband source localization and our method. In section 3.2 we carefully define our use\ncase e.g., we define the broadband condition for source localization. We then develop our\nsignal model in section 3.3, as well as some of the tools used in our estimation algorithm.\nWe formally state our algorithms in section 3.4. Narrowband experiments are overviewed\nin section 3.5 and broadband experiments are given in section 3.6. In section 3.7 we show\nhow the algorithms can naturally be extended to a multi-sensor array scenario, localizing\nsources in time and space. Finally, section 3.8 concludes the chapter.\n25--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Iterative Broadband Source Localization (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/364658746_Iterative_Broadband_Source_Localization?_tp=eyJjb250ZXh0Ijp7InBhZ2UiOiJzY2llbnRpZmljQ29udHJpYnV0aW9ucyIsInByZXZpb3VzUGFnZSI6bnVsbCwic3ViUGFnZSI6bnVsbH19)\nciteturn225search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; As a ﬁnal note, our derivation of the union of Slepian space model followed from the assumption ... spectral leakage, we mean the phenomenon where, due to truncation eﬀects, the DFT of a ﬁnite\n\n3.2 Spectral leakage of broadband signals\n\nA powerful beneﬁt to the Slepian subspace model is that it is robust to “spectral leakage.” By\n\nspectral leakage, we mean the phenomenon where, due to truncation eﬀects, the DFT of a ﬁnite\n\n7\n\nx 1[n] = 3 cos( 2 π·2\n\n100 n)−2 cos( 2 π·5\n\n100 n) + 4 cos(2 π·9\n\n100 n), n = 0,1,...,99\n\nx 2[n] = 3 cos( 2 π·1.6\n\n100 n)−2 cos( 2 π·5.1\n\n100 n) + 4 cos(2 π·8.5\n\n100 n), n = 0,1,...,99\n\nFigure 2: (Top row) A plot of the magnitudes of the DFT coeﬃcients (top left) and the Slepian basis\n\ncoeﬃcients with N= 100 and W=1\n\n10 (top right) for the signal x 1[n], which is a sum of three sinusoids\n\nat grid frequencies. (Bottom row) A plot of the magnitudes of the DFT coeﬃcients (bottom left) and the\n\nSlepian basis coeﬃcients with N= 100 and W=1\n\n10 (bottom ri